WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:04.100
-Our first guest tonight
is an Oscar-winning actress,

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.834
a best-selling author,
and the founder and CEO

00:00:06.834 --> 00:00:07.968
of Goop.

00:00:07.968 --> 00:00:09.300
Goop's latest product,

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.868
the G.Tox Reset kit,
is available now.

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:15.234
Here is Gwyneth Paltrow,
everybody!

00:00:15.234 --> 00:00:18.634
Ohh, my gosh! Yeah! Come on!

00:00:18.634 --> 00:00:20.334
I'm so happy to see you.

00:00:20.334 --> 00:00:23.601
Mwah, mwah, mwah, mwah.
I'm so happy to see you, bud.

00:00:23.601 --> 00:00:25.901
Thanks so much
for coming on the show.

00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:30.067
-I'm so happy to be here in this
weird, surreal, virtual way.

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.000
-Yeah. Yeah, exactly.
What is even going down?

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.267
How is the family?
How is Moses and Apple doing?

00:00:36.267 --> 00:00:38.534
-Oh, they're so good.
Thank you for asking.

00:00:38.534 --> 00:00:41.667
Yeah, they're nearly 18 and 16.

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.400
Moses is about to get
his driver's license.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.400
-That is insane to me.
-It's all happening.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:48.667
-It's all happening, bud.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:49.934
-How are your girls?

00:00:49.934 --> 00:00:51.501
-They're so cute. 8 and 7.

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:53.934
And just awesome
and just so fun and creative.

00:00:53.934 --> 00:00:56.100
And I love it.
It's just the best thing ever.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.467
I ran into Moses over the
summer, by the way, with Chris.

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:01.501
And he's --
Not only is he a stud,

00:01:01.501 --> 00:01:03.934
but he's one of the nicest kids
I ever met.

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:06.133
So great job with that guy.
-Thank you.

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.567
-And give my best to Apple, too.
I never bring her up enough.

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:11.634
I don't want her to be like,
"Does Jimmy not like me?"

00:01:11.634 --> 00:01:13.434
Because I love that kid.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:16.234
I've known you
for so many years,

00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:18.901
and I remember
when you started Goop.

00:01:18.901 --> 00:01:21.100
And it was --
I was like, "Oh, I get it.

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.200
Goop. GP.
Gwyneth Paltrow. Goop."

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.534
And it was, like -- I think
it started in your kitchen,

00:01:25.534 --> 00:01:27.400
I want to say,
and you were sending out --

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.400
It was just newsletters?
-Yeah.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.434
-And now you're the founder
and CEO of this giant company.

00:01:32.434 --> 00:01:39.067
I mean, what is the best part
of running Goop?

00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:40.701
-Oh, my gosh.

00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:42.200
I think the best part --

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.701
and I know it sounds,
like, counterintuitive --

00:01:44.701 --> 00:01:47.601
but the best part
is how challenging it is,

00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:49.667
because I learn so much
all the time.

00:01:49.667 --> 00:01:52.801
And I think,
if you start in one career

00:01:52.801 --> 00:01:57.501
and you kind of have the
gumption to kind of jump ship

00:01:57.501 --> 00:01:59.667
and do something else,

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:02.901
not only are you sort of
working against, like,

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:04.501
what people think
you're capable of,

00:02:04.501 --> 00:02:06.400
but what you think
you're capable of.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.901
And so you just
are in this position

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:10.534
where you're learning
all the time.

00:02:10.534 --> 00:02:15.200
And it's just -- It's scary, but
it's also incredibly fulfilling.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.100
And I feel really alive,

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.901
and I really love
the people I work with.

00:02:18.901 --> 00:02:20.834
We have such a great team
at Goop,

00:02:20.834 --> 00:02:23.100
and that's probably
the best part, is the people.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.167
-Yeah, everyone loves
working for you and with you,

00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:27.501
by the way.

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:29.033
Everyone I've talked to --
They love it.

00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:31.601
What are the offices like?
Are you in the office now?

00:02:31.601 --> 00:02:34.601
Or are you in a cloud somewhere?
Are you --

00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:36.167
-We're in a cloud.

00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:38.868
We were so excited to all
go back and be together

00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:42.767
and get back to our
kombucha on tap and whatever.

00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:44.200
-Yeah.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:47.934
-But we're all back in our
closets/offices -- home offices.

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.501
-Zooming.

00:02:49.501 --> 00:02:52.434
-And hoping that, you know,
at some point this quarter,

00:02:52.434 --> 00:02:54.367
we'll be able to go back
and be together,

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:56.033
but I guess
it remains to be seen.

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:59.400
-I went to a Goop store.
You have stores.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.234
-Thank you. We have a couple.

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:05.400
-I know. I didn't know this.
I was like, "Wait. Goop store?"

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.434
So I went in --
I went in with Nancy.

00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:09.868
And I will say this --
It was in Sag Harbor.

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:12.434
There were the nicest people
working there.

00:03:12.434 --> 00:03:14.033
They were so nice.

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:16.501
And I was like --
And it was so curated

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:19.701
and clean and beautiful,
and every little thing is cool.

00:03:19.701 --> 00:03:24.067
And I was like, "Oh.
This is just so well done."

00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:26.667
And you do this stuff,
and you go,

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:29.801
"Okay, this is, like,
good for you."

00:03:29.801 --> 00:03:31.934
And then you do edgy stuff.

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:35.033
But then you do stuff that
has, again, helps everyone

00:03:35.033 --> 00:03:36.534
and has a great purpose.

00:03:36.534 --> 00:03:39.634
You've teamed up with the ACLU
with a new candle.

00:03:39.634 --> 00:03:41.534
You're not making any money
off this.

00:03:41.534 --> 00:03:44.267
The money goes to ACLU,
all the profits.

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:46.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:51.200
And I want to show --
I want to show the candle.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.801
And I don't know. I just --

00:03:52.801 --> 00:03:56.133
Maybe I think it's better if you
tell everyone what it's called.

00:03:56.133 --> 00:03:58.734
-Yes, the candle is in honor

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:01.634
of the anniversary
of "Roe v. Wade."

00:04:01.634 --> 00:04:04.334
We made a candle called
"Hands Off My Vagina."

00:04:04.334 --> 00:04:05.968
-[ Cheers and applause ]
-They love it.

00:04:05.968 --> 00:04:07.901
[ Laughs ]

00:04:07.901 --> 00:04:12.667
But you know what? It's edgy.
But that's exactly --

00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:16.067
It gets people talking,
and it gets people, you know,

00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:17.901
to donate to a great cause.

00:04:17.901 --> 00:04:23.467
-You know, I think we are very
pro-woman at Goop, obviously,

00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:27.667
and we really do believe
that women should have agency

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.801
to make whatever choice
they want to make in their lives

00:04:29.801 --> 00:04:33.000
in any area,
and so we were happy to be able

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.934
to partner with the ACLU
and do this.

00:04:34.934 --> 00:04:36.334
It's really exciting.

00:04:36.334 --> 00:04:37.868
-How do you come up
with new products?

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:39.267
Do you just write them all down?

00:04:39.267 --> 00:04:41.367
Do you record them on your phone
in the middle of the night?

00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:42.901
Do you go like,
"Oh, this could be an idea

00:04:42.901 --> 00:04:44.801
or this could be something"?

00:04:44.801 --> 00:04:47.100
-I usually get them sort of
in a lightning flash,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.901
like I did with this candle.

00:04:49.901 --> 00:04:52.334
I was having a dinner one night,

00:04:52.334 --> 00:04:55.167
and we were talking about
freedoms in this country.

00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:56.934
And it just sort of hit me.

00:04:56.934 --> 00:04:59.400
I was like, "Oh, I would love
to make a candle like that."

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:03.000
And sometimes they come from
collaboration with the team,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.734
and our team can come up
with, like, the funniest,

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:09.000
cleverest names for products.
-Yeah.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.968
-So it's really --
It's a great process, you know,

00:05:11.968 --> 00:05:14.167
to be able to have that
creative freedom at work

00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:16.501
and to do things
you know will be edgy

00:05:16.501 --> 00:05:18.100
and, like, move culture forward

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:21.067
and get people talking
for a good cause.

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:22.634
So we love it.

00:05:22.634 --> 00:05:26.501
-Now you've branched out
to even, like, TV now.

00:05:26.501 --> 00:05:28.601
You're doing TV shows.
You're doing podcasts.

00:05:28.601 --> 00:05:30.801
It's become the biggest --

00:05:30.801 --> 00:05:33.133
It's become giant,
as big as you like, really.

00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:36.133
Do people come to up you and,
like, ask you for advice now?

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:40.133
Like, "what can I do?
How can I start my business?"

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:42.300
-Yeah, they do,
and I love that, too,

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.367
because I, obviously,
learned on the job.

00:05:44.367 --> 00:05:46.100
And I didn't even
finish college,

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:48.000
and I didn't go to
business school or anything.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.434
So I've made so many mistakes,

00:05:50.434 --> 00:05:53.467
and it's probably taken me a lot
longer to build my business

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:56.067
than it would somebody who had
done it a million times before

00:05:56.067 --> 00:05:58.300
or had, like,
incredible experience.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:02.200
And so I really want to save
other entrepreneurs that I know

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.868
from making the same mistakes
that I made along the way.

00:06:04.868 --> 00:06:09.501
So I actually speak to a number
of entrepreneurs weekly,

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:12.834
and I invest in companies
and help however I can,

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:14.767
you know,
if I believe in a product.

00:06:14.767 --> 00:06:17.834
Because I really do think
that the entrepreneurial spirit

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:20.400
is such an important part
of being an American

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.067
and the freedom to invent.

00:06:22.067 --> 00:06:26.801
And so I love being able to get
that chance to help where I can.

00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:31.133
-Yeah. I want to talk
about your new detox kit,

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:33.000
called G.Tox, when we come back.
-Oh, yeah.

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.667
-More with Gwyneth Paltrow,
everybody.

00:06:34.667 --> 00:06:36.467
Stick around. Come on back.

